Code Quality

Keep any refactor or code additions simple, readable, and uncomplicated.
Keep code inline with the current style of existing code.
Keep things concise.
Combine methods where it makes sense and will not break existing code.
Prefer editing existing functions over creating new ones with similar purpose.
Match existing naming conventions exactly — don't introduce a new casing style or naming pattern.
Don't add comments explaining obvious code; comment only non-obvious logic or decisions.
Avoid premature abstraction — don't generalize code for hypothetical future use cases.

After any refactor or new code: delete everything that isn't 
required. No speculative abstractions, no convenience wrappers 
without multiple callers, no duplicated logic, no extra parameters. 
The goal is the minimum correct implementation. Defend every line.

If a fix reintroduces code or logic that was recently removed or
changed, stop and flag it instead of proceeding. Do not silently
cycle back to a previous state. Circular changes are a signal to
ask for clarification, not to keep iterating.

Never modify code that is not directly related to the current task.
If existing code works and is not causing the problem being solved,
leave it alone. Refactoring working code requires explicit instruction.

Testing & Validation

When modifying logic, note what should be manually tested afterward.
Don't write tests unless explicitly asked, but flag when a change seems risky enough to warrant one.


## Error Handling
Do not add generic try/catch blocks or swallow exceptions without
explicit instruction. Error handling must be specific to the actual
failure modes of the code. Never catch an exception just to log
and re-throw it — that adds noise without value.

## Naming
Do not rename variables, methods, or classes unless the current
name is factually wrong or directly conflicts with the task.
Renaming working, understood code is scope creep.

## Comments
Do not add comments that describe what the code is doing —
the code should speak for itself. Only add comments for:
1. Why a non-obvious decision was made
2. Known limitations or gotchas
3. Explicit instruction to document

## Testing
Do not modify existing passing tests unless the task explicitly
requires it. If a refactor breaks a passing test, that is a signal
the refactor is wrong — not that the test needs updating.
New tests must test behavior, not implementation details.

## Dependencies
Do not introduce new libraries, packages, or dependencies to solve
a problem that can be reasonably solved with what already exists.
Every new dependency must be explicitly approved.

## Configuration & Environment
Never hardcode values that belong in configuration or environment
variables. Never modify existing config values unless directly
required by the task.

## Security
Never log, print, or expose sensitive values (tokens, passwords,
keys, PII) even in debug or error output. Do not add backdoors,
debug endpoints, or temporary auth bypasses — even for testing.

## Git / Change Hygiene
Keep changes focused and atomic. Do not bundle unrelated fixes
into the same change. If you notice something unrelated that
should be fixed, flag it separately rather than fixing it in place.

## When Stuck or Uncertain
If the correct solution is unclear, ask before implementing.
Do not produce speculative code and hope it fits. A wrong
implementation costs more time to unwind than a clarifying
question costs to ask.

## Tone on Disagreement
If you believe the requested approach is flawed, say so once
with a specific reason. Then implement what was asked unless
it is a clear security or correctness issue. Do not silently
implement a different approach than what was requested.

## Simplicity & Minimal Code
Prefer the solution with the fewest lines that is still readable.
Do not add wrapper methods, helper classes, or intermediate variables
unless they are used more than once or dramatically aid readability.

Never add code for future flexibility, anticipated use cases,
or "this might be useful later." Only implement what is
explicitly required right now.

Before adding any new method, class, or utility: search the
existing codebase for equivalent logic. If it exists, use or
extend it. Never create a parallel implementation.

After any refactor or new code: delete everything that isn't
required. No speculative abstractions, no convenience wrappers
without multiple callers, no duplicated logic, no extra parameters.
The goal is the minimum correct implementation. Defend every line.

Do not touch working code outside the scope of the current task.
Every change to existing logic must answer: "what was wrong with
this before?" If the answer is nothing — revert it.

## Circular Refactor Prevention
Before refactoring any existing working code, confirm:
1. What is broken or insufficient about the current implementation?
2. What measurable improvement does this change make?
3. Does this change risk breaking anything that currently works?
If question 1 has no concrete answer, do not make the change.

If a fix reintroduces code or logic that was recently removed or
changed, stop and flag it instead of proceeding. Do not silently
cycle back to a previous state. Circular changes are a signal to
ask for clarification, not to keep iterating.